<Project>

  <!--
    Windows and WSL build the same checkout over /mnt/d, and until now into the
    same bin/ and obj/. That is not merely untidy, it fails silently: after a
    Linux run, a Windows `dotnet test` that skips the build produced no output
    at all - no error, no summary, exit code 0 - because the test host and the
    .deps.json it reads had been written for the other platform. A tool that
    says nothing is worse than one that fails, and this one had to be caught by
    noticing that a run which used to print thirty lines printed none.

    UseArtifactsOutput moves every project in this tree into one directory, and
    the condition gives each platform its own. The two no longer meet.

    Why "build/" and not "artifacts/", which is what the SDK calls this by
    default: nightly.yml uploads everything under `artifacts/` for the .trx
    files and the peer logs, and pointing the build output there would sweep the
    whole compiled tree into that upload.

    This file sits at the root of the superproject, so it reaches the submodule
    projects as well - which is the point, since libs/Hermod and the rest share
    their bin/ across the same border. A standalone checkout of Ratatoskr or
    XMPPConsole walks up past its own root and never finds this, so their own
    builds and their own CI are untouched.

    (No double hyphen anywhere above: an XML comment may not contain one, and
    naming the flag that started this cost a build.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseArtifactsOutput>true</UseArtifactsOutput>
    <ArtifactsPath Condition=" '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' ">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\windows</ArtifactsPath>
    <ArtifactsPath Condition=" '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' ">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/linux</ArtifactsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
